     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi all,</div><div><br></div><div>New user, looking into INLA as a possible mixed modelling software, due to needing to work with some mixed-models with random effects with many levels. I&#39;m trying to track down why INLA seems to be giving me a very different result than lme4 does. What I&#39;m trying to figure out is modelling correlated random slopes and intercepts. When I use INLA, I&#39;m currently getting a really strongly negative correlation between the random slopes and intercepts by subject, when it should be really strong positive correlation. I&#39;m trying to figure out if I&#39;ve made an error in setting up the formula, or if it&#39;s maybe something to do with the priors on random slope correlatioms in INLA.<br><br>Thanks,</div><div>Bryor<br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">INLA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">### simulation params</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>n </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>n_items </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">50</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>n_subjects </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">30</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>item_sd </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>b0 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>b1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>subject_intercept_sd </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>subject_slope_sd </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>subject_intercept_slope_correlation </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">9</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>noise_sd </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>m </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subject_intercept_slope_correlation</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subject_intercept_slope_correlation</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nrow </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>slope_cov_mat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subject_intercept_sd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> subject_slope_sd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> m </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subject_intercept_sd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> subject_slope_sd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">### random variables</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>subjects </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sample</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n_subjects</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> replace </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>items </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sample</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n_items</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> replace </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>item_random_intercepts </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n_items</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> item_sd</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>subject_random_effects </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> MASS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mvrnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n_subjects</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> slope_cov_mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b0 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">b1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> subject_random_effects</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subjects</span><span sty