   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r<br>the first thing is to make sure that you have implemented it correct,\r<br>for which you can keep noise_sd low and fixed, and you should be able to\r<br>get back the model you put in. \r<br>\r<br>Use Gaussian response, as then there are no approximation errors, just\r<br>some numerical integration approximations. \r<br>\r<br>the next step is to apply it to data, for which priors can make a\r<br>difference, as there are alternative interpretations of the same data\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Sun, 2020-12-20 at 23:16 -0800, Bryor Snefjella wrote:\r<br>&gt; Lowering the sd of the noise did flip the correlation. How does that\r<br>&gt; translate into practice in real data analysis, where the sd of the\r<br>&gt; noise isn&#39;t known?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Sunday, December 20, 2020 at 11:05:23 PM UTC-8, Helpdesk wrote:\r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; the first step here, is always to fix noise_sd to a low value, like\r<br>&gt; &gt; 1E-3\r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; control.family=list(hyper=list(prec=list(initial=log(1/noise_sd^2),\r<br>&gt; &gt; fixed=TRUE))\r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; On Sun, 2020-12-20 at 23:03 -0800, Bryor Snefjella wrote:\r<br>&gt; &gt; &gt; noise_sd\r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>\r<br>-- \r<br>Håvard Rue\r<br><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>\r<br>\r<br>"
